{"name":"Green Lizard","hex":"#a7f432","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"green lizard","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Synonym of European green lizard, eastern green lizard (Lacerta viridis).","Synonym of western green lizard (Lacerta bilineata), of western Europe."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green_lizard"}],"entries":[{"title":"Green Lizard (band)","blocks":[{"text":"Green Lizard is a band from Tilburg, Netherlands that plays rock and roll, with influences from grunge, punk rock, hard rock and metal."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Green_Lizard%2C_band.jpg/330px-Green_Lizard%2C_band.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_Lizard_(band)","wikidata":"Q2615664"},{"title":"European green lizard","blocks":[{"text":"The European green lizard is a large lizard distributed across European mid-latitudes from Slovenia and eastern Austria to as far east as the Black Sea coasts of Ukraine and Turkey. It is often seen sunning on rocks or lawns, or sheltering amongst bushes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Lacerta_viridis_-_male_01.JPG/330px-Lacerta_viridis_-_male_01.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_green_lizard","wikidata":"Q307047"},{"title":"Lizard","blocks":[{"text":"Lizard is the common name used for all squamate reptiles that are not snakes, encompassing over 7,000 species. They have a cosmopolitan distribution, ranging across all continents except Antarctica, as well as most oceanic island chains. Extant lizard species vary in size from the 13.5 mm (0.53 in) nano-chameleon and the 14–18 mm (0.55–0.71 in) Jaragua dwarf gecko to the 3-meter-long Komodo dragon."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Hemidactylus_platyurus_%28Flat-tailed_House_Gecko%29_on_white_background%2C_focus_stacking.jpg/330px-Hemidactylus_platyurus_%28Flat-tailed_House_Gecko%29_on_white_background%2C_focus_stacking.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lizard","wikidata":"Q15879"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Lacerta_viridis_-_female_01.JPG/500px-Lacerta_viridis_-_female_01.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Lacerta_viridis_-_female_01.JPG/1280px-Lacerta_viridis_-_female_01.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Lacer